WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.400
When it comes to something
as life-and-death important

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.120
as what to feed ourselves
and our families,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.760
we should rely not on
anecdotes, but on facts.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.040
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.840
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.640
Today we explore the findings
of five massive new trials

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.400
of the effectiveness of
fish oil supplements.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.440
Thanks in part to the

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.560
American Heart
Association's recommendation

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.400
that individuals at high risk
for heart disease

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.600
ask their physicians

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.600
about omega-3
fish oil supplementation,

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.480
fish oil pills have grown into
a multibillion-dollar industry.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.000
We now consume more
than a hundred thousand tons

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.480
of fish oil every year.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.400
But what does the science say?

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.280
Are the purported benefits
of fish oil supplementation

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.480
for the prevention and treatment
of heart disease promising,

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.960
or just a fish tale?

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.440
The scientific sea change

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.640
occurred a little over
a decade ago

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.840
when a systematic review
and meta-analysis

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:02.000
published in the Journal of
the American Medical Association

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.000
looked at all the best
randomized clinical trials

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.000
evaluating the effects
of omega-3 fats on life span,

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.800
cardiac death, sudden death,
heart attack, and stroke.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.160
These included studies
not only on fish oil supplements

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.560
but also studies
on the effects

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.480
of advising people
to eat more oily fish.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.080
Overall, the researchers found

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.080
no protective benefit
for overall mortality,

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.320
heart disease mortality,
sudden cardiac death,

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.760
heart attack, or stroke.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:31.880
What about for someone who had
already had a heart attack

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:33.680
and is trying
to prevent another one?

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.960
Still no benefit was found.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.520
Since then, another five-dozen
randomized, controlled trials

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:41.560
have been compiled,

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.240
and the most extensive
systematic assessment confirmed

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.880
that increasing the intake
of fish fats (EPA and DHA)

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.240
had "little or no effect

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.120
on deaths
and cardiovascular events."

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.000
Longevity experiments on mice

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.040
found no benefits for aging
or lifespan either.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.240
Where did we even get this idea

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.120
that the omega-3 fats in fish
and fish oil supplements

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.920
were good for us?

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.600
There was a notion that Eskimos

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.080
were protected
from heart disease,

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.680
but that appears to be
a complete myth.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.200
Some early studies,
however, looked promising.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.000
For example,
the famous DART trial

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.440
from the 1980s
involving 2,000 men

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.960
found that those
advised to eat fatty fish

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.080
had a 29 percent reduction
in mortality.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.480
That's impressive,

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.240
no wonder the study
got a lot of attention.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.360
Fish oil
became a whale of a story

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.000
that got bigger
with every telling.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.880
But people seem to have
forgotten about the sequel,

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.160
the DART-2 trial,
which found the exact opposite.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.400
Run by the same group
of researchers,

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:44.720
the DART-2 trial
was an even bigger study,

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:46.240
3,000 men,

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.440
but this time,

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.280
participants advised
to eat oily fish

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.640
and particularly those
who were supplied

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.120
with fish oil capsules

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.520
had a higher risk
of cardiac death.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:56.920
Concerns about the potential

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:00.280
pro-arrhythmic (irregular
rhythm inducing) effects

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:03.640
of fish oil has led to reviews
containing titles like,

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.040
"Can Fish Oil Kill You?"

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:09.440
But again, little or no change
in overall mortality risk

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.360
has been found when all
the studies are put together.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.240
Beyond just concerns
about persistent pollutants,

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:18.120
such as dioxins and PCBs
in fish oil supplements,

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.600
even in "distilled"
fish oil supplements,

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.680
oxidation by-products
have been found

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.000
throughout the commercial
fish oil supplements industry.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.080
Surveys of popular omega-3
supplements in Asia, Europe,

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:33.760
the Middle East,
Australia, and North America

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.520
found that 24-92 percent

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:38.760
exceeded the voluntary
safety standards

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.360
set by the industry,

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.840
there's no legal limit,
at least in the United States.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.920
The country of origin
didn't seem to matter,

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.160
perhaps because
most fish oil products

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.960
sold globally
are evidently sourced

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:55.160
from the same region (off the
west coast of South America).

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.280
Surprisingly, the best-before
date also wasn't helpful.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.400
In this study, where nine
out of ten supplements

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.280
exceeded safety standards,

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.800
they were all tested
at least nine months before

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:08.480
the best-before dates.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.560
The more expensive ones
were more contaminated,

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.280
but this is thought to be
an artifact of higher cost

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.880
relating to
higher concentrations.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.160
Those obtained from algae

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.880
were also better
than those from fish.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.560
The United States has
the highest sales of fish oil

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:24.520
in the world,

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.800
exceeding a billion dollars
a year.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:29.040
When three top-selling
fish oil supplements

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.360
in the U.S. were tested,

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.560
they all exceeded
the recommended maximum levels

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.320
of oxidation products,

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.480
though expanded testing
to four dozen U.S. supplements

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.040
only found 52 percent
exceeded the limits.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.800
And these two tests
may give false positives

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:45.840
due to added colors or flavors.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.680
A USDA study found
that more than 70 percent

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:52.800
contained less EPA and/or DHA
than stated on their labels,

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.040
but the greater concern

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.760
is not what fish oil supplements
are lacking

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:59.120
but rather what's not listed
on the label,

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:02.160
like the contaminants,
oxidation by-products,

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:03.440
and saturated fat,

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.880
which may explain
why fish-based omega-3s

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.280
raise LDL cholesterol levels

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.480
compared to
plant-based omega-3s.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.520
The public health implications
of consuming oxidized fish oil

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:16.360
are unclear, however.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:19.160
Animal experiments
suggest chronic exposure

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:21.600
can cause inflammation,
organ toxicity,

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.520
and accelerated atherosclerosis,

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:28.040
but no long-term human studies
have been done.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:29.440
Ironically, one of the reasons

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.280
there haven't been
more clinical studies

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:34.360
are the ethical concerns
knowingly exposing people

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:36.120
to oxidized fats;

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.160
yet, that experiment
is being carried out en masse

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.880
as we speak
in bathroom medicine cabinets

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:44.040
around the world.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:45.840
After putting
all the studies together,

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:49.080
the authors of the landmark
AMA journal meta-analysis

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.200
concluded that there was
no longer justification

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.840
for the use of omega-3s
in everyday clinical practice.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.600
What should doctors do
when their patients follow

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.280
the American
Heart Association's advice

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:03.240
and inquire
about fish oil supplements?

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.000
As the director
of Lipids and Metabolism

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.960
at Mount Sinai's
Cardiovascular Institute put it:

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:10.360
"Given this and other
negative meta-analyses,

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:13.520
our job as doctors
should be to stop

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.760
highly marketed
fish oil supplementation

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:17.720
to all our patients."

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:20.680
The research momentum was
already in full swing, though,

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.280
and five massive new trials
have been recently published

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.480
randomizing tens of thousands
to various formulations

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:30.040
of fish oil versus placebo.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:32.920
Four out of five flopped.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:36.040
The so-called ASCEND trial,
the OMEMI trial,

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:38.680
the STRENGTH trial,
and the VITAL trial

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:42.040
all failed to reduce deaths
or cardiovascular events,

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:44.400
but one final nail
in the coffin

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:45.920
appeared to be loose.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:49.000
The REDUCE-IT trial
didn't significantly reduce

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:50.000
overall mortality

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.800
but did reduce a composite
of cardiovascular events

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:55.640
by 25 percent.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:58.600
It used
a prescription-only dose of EPA,

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:02.440
equivalent to eating about
150 cans of tuna fish a day.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:05.880
The problem
is the placebo they used.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:09.960
Unlike other trials that used
more neutral oils like corn oil,

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.280
the drug company
that funded REDUCE-IT

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:15.400
to promote its own product
chose mineral oil

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:18.800
for the control group,
a classic drug industry trick

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:21.080
where you try to make
your product look better

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:23.480
by comparing it
against something worse.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:26.800
And, indeed the mineral oil
quote-unquote "placebo"

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:29.180
increased LDL cholesterol
and caused more than

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:31.680
a 30 percent increase
in C-reactive protein.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:35.520
No wonder the fish oil
looked good in comparison.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.880
It's possible some fish oil
formulation will eventually

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.320
prove to be helpful,
but for now, meta-analyses

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:44.160
"unequivocally demonstrate

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:46.800
that there is no
cardiovascular benefit"

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.120
to over-the-counter
fish oil supplements.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:51.920
In our next story,

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:56.240
we look at how a study of 13 over-the-counter
children’s fish oil supplements

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:00.449
found that all were contaminated
with PCB pollutants.

00:08:00.449 --> 00:08:02.440
A number of so-called case-control
studies like this one

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.200
have found that giving kids
cod liver oil supplements

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:09.040
may increase their risk
of asthma later in life.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:12.400
Case-control studies are done
by asking about past behavior

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:16.240
in cases (those with asthma) versus
controls (those without asthma)

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:20.960
to see if certain past behaviors are
more common among the disease group.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:23.960
The problem is you’re asking people to
remember what they were doing years ago,

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:27.160
and most people can’t remember what
they had for breakfast last week,

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:29.840
and you can’t rule out something
called reverse causation.

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:32.000
Maybe cod liver oil
didn’t lead to the asthma,

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.680
maybe the asthma led to
the use of cod liver oil.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:38.640
So it would be nice
to see a cohort study.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:41.000
You take people without
asthma at the beginning

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:44.360
and you see if those that you
know are taking cod liver oil

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:45.920
are more likely to develop it.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:49.320
That gets around the problems of
recall bias and reverse causation.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:49.960
And here it is.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:53.740
17,000 people free of asthma, we know
who's taking cod liver oil and who isn't,

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:57.000
and then we see who gets
asthma over the next 11 years.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.120
And they indeed found that
cod liver oil intake

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:03.640
was significantly associated
with the development of asthma.

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:05.600
They think it may be the
excessive vitamin A

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.840
in the cod liver oil that
was causing the problem,

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:12.920
but there are a number of things in fish oil
we may not want our children exposed to.

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:17.000
Researchers recently looked at 13
over-the-counter children’s dietary supplements

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:21.320
containing fish oil to assess
potential exposure to PCBs,

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:25.120
toxic industrial pollutants that
have contaminated our oceans.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:29.120
PCBs were detected in ALL products.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:32.640
Could you just stick to the supplements
made from the small, short-lived fish

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:37.880
like anchovies instead of big predator fish
like tuna, or use the purified fish oils?

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:41.440
No! They found no significant
difference in PCB levels

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:44.880
between the supplements labeled
as molecularly distilled

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:48.520
or however high up the
food chain the fish were.

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:50.720
So while children’s dietary
supplements containing

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:54.640
the long-chain omega-3’s from fish oils
may CLAIM to benefit young consumers,

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:58.800
daily ingestion of these products may
provide a vector for contaminant exposure

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.880
that may off-set the
positive health effects.

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:04.120
What positive health effects
are they talking about?

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:10.200
Well, infants given DHA-fortified
formula may have better development

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:14.840
of their eyes and brains compared to
infants getting non DHA-fortified formula.

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:17.840
DHAs are one these long-chain
omega-3 fatty acids.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:19.720
But what was the
source of the DHA?

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:22.800
Not fish!
It was algae-derived DHA.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:27.960
So you can get the benefits of
omega 3’s without the contaminant risks.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.000
Of course, breast milk
is the gold standard,

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.760
significantly better than either
of the formula fed infants.

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:40.200
So the best source
of omega-3’s is mom.

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:40.880
Finally today,

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:44.640
we discover how advice to
eat oily fish, or take fish oil,

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:49.280
is no longer supported by the
balance of available evidence.

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:52.160
Are purported benefits of
fish oil supplementation

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:54.120
for the prevention and treatment
of heart disease

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.480
just a "fish tale"?

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:57.480
Thanks to recommendations
like this

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:59.440
from the American Heart Association,

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:01.760
that individuals at high
risk for heart disease

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:05.000
ask their physicians about
fish oil supplementation,

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:07.760
it's grown into a multi-
billion dollar industry.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:12.760
We now consume over 100,000 tons
of fish oil every year.

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:15.800
But what does the
latest science say?

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:18.480
A systematic review and
meta-analysis published in

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:20.520
the Journal of the
American Medical Association

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:23.400
looked at all the best
randomized clinical trials

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:25.160
evaluating the effects
of omega-3s

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:29.680
on lifespan, cardiac death, sudden death,
heart attack, and stroke.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:32.520
Either advice to eat
more oily fish

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:35.440
or to take
fish oil capsules.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:37.360
What did they find?

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:39.920
Overall they found NO
protective benefit

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:43.200
for overall mortality,
heart disease mortality,

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.960
sudden cardiac death,
heart attack, or stroke.

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:48.520
What about for those who've already
had a heart attack, though,

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:50.880
and are trying to
prevent another one?

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:54.200
Still NO benefit.

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:56.400
Where did we even
get this idea

00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:59.520
that omega-3s were
good for the heart?

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:01.400
Well, if you look at some
of the older studies,

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:02.960
the results
looked promising,

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:05.560
For example, the
famous DART trial

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:08.400
back in the 80s,
involving 2,000 men–

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:12.520
those advised to eat fatty fish
had a 29% reduction in mortality.

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:13.880
Pretty impressive.

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:16.320
No wonder it got a
lot of attention.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:20.400
But people seem to have
forgotten about the sequel–

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:22.720
the DART II trial.

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:24.120
Same group of
researchers

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:26.600
and even bigger study – 3,000 men.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:28.960
And those advised
to eat oily fish,

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:31.480
and particularly those supplied
with fish oil capsules

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:35.280
had a HIGHER risk
of cardiac death.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.400
Put all the
studies together...

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:39.920
and there's NO
justification...

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:42.480
for the use of omega-3s as
a structured intervention

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:44.080
in everyday
clinical practice

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:48.080
or for guidelines supporting
more dietary omega-3s.

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:49.960
So what should
doctors say

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:53.440
when their patients follow the
American Heart Association advice

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:57.080
to ask them about
fish oil supplements?

00:12:57.080 --> 00:13:01.920
Well, given the new meta-analysis
and other negative meta-analyses,

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:04.920
our job as doctors
should be to STOP

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:10.334
highly marketed fish oil supplementation
in ALL of our patients.

